reover, J10 is also on White’s potential diagonal: from I8-J9, next is K10, not J10.\n\nJ10 is not aligned with I8-J9.\n\nI8 (9,8), J9 (10,9) → next is K10 (11,10)\n\nJ10 is (10,10) → not on that line.\n\nSo playing J10 doesn\'t help White’s offense.\n\nWhereas I9, if played by White, blocks Black and may help White build vertically or horizontally.\n\nBut I9 is not on White’s current lines.\n\nStill, between I9 and J10, which is better for blocking?\n\n- Playing **I9** blocks Black’s 3 at the immediate continuation — prevents 4-in-a-row threat.\n- Playing **J10** blocks the far end, but allows Black to play I9 and create a 4-in-a-row (F6-G7-H8-I9), which although blocked at J10, is still dangerous if there were escape routes — but there aren\'t.\n\nBut here’s the key: **an open four is a winning threat**. But if Black plays I9, and J10 is blocked, then it\'s not open — it\'s a "dead" four.\n\nBut what if Black has another way? No.\n\nHowever, **if White plays J10, Black can instead play I9, and now has a 4-stone line, but cannot extend** — so no immediate threat.\n\nBut White gains time.\n\nBut is there a difference?\n\nYes: if White plays **I9**, it prevents Black from getting 4-in-a-row entirely — because I9 is missing.\n\nWith I9 blocked, the best Black can do is 3-in-a-row.\n\nWhereas if White plays J10, Black can play I9 to make 4-in-a-row (though capped).\n\nBut in Gomoku, a **four-in-a-row that is blocked on one end is not immediately lethal**, but it can be part of a trap.\n\nBut more importantly: **does Black have a double threat?**\n\nNot really.\n\nBut standard strategy: **block at the most critical point**.\n\nThe strongest block is to prevent the creation of an open four.\n\nBy playing **I9**, White prevents Black from forming even a four.\n\nBy playing **J10**, White allows Black to form a four (albeit closed).\n\nTherefore, **I9 is the superior block**.\n\nAdditionally, I9 is closer to White’s stones — might help in building influence.\n\nNow, is there a move that both attacks and defends?\n\nFor example, can White play a move that creates a 3-in-a-row while blocking?\n\nNot obviously.\n\nWhite’s best offensive move is K10 to continue from J9, but that doesn’t block.\n\nOr L11, but too far.\n\nNo dual-purpose move.\n\nTherefore, priority is defense.\n\nThus, **I9 is the best move**.\n\nIt is unoccupied (not in the list), so legal.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nEvaluation: Black has a strong diagonal threat with F6-G7-H8, aiming to extend to I9 and J10 for five. I9 is the critical point to block this progression. White must prevent Black from forming an open four. Although J10 also blocks, it allows Black to establish a four-in-a-row (even if capped). Playing I9 stops the threat earlier and is more effective. No immediate winning move for White, so defense is paramount.\n\nReason: Blocking at I9 neutralizes Black’s 3-in-a-row threat most efficiently and prevents future development, while maintaining balance.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 9}'}
